  "response": "Adjudicating between drug-induced liver injury and an underlying viral or autoimmune hepatitis turns on biochemistry, timing, and the systematic exclusion of competing causes. The patient developed jaundice three weeks after starting amoxicillin-clavulanate. Serum alanine aminotransferase was elevated to more than five times the upper limit of normal, and that degree of elevation led directly to the pattern calculation: the R value is calculated by dividing the alanine aminotransferase level by the alkaline phosphatase level, each expressed as a multiple of its upper limit of normal. As a result of that arithmetic, an R value greater than 5 defines a hepatocellular pattern of liver injury. That the injury is hepatocellular in pattern is evidence that the jaundice arose from direct injury to hepatocytes. At least one of two mechanisms is at work in a presentation of this kind \u2014 direct damage to hepatocytes or impaired bile flow within the liver \u2014 and perhaps both.\n\nSerology then narrows the field. After the aminotransferase elevation was documented, clinicians tested for hepatitis A IgM, hepatitis B surface antigen, and hepatitis C RNA. A positive hepatitis C IgG antibody result confirms acute hepatitis C infection. Hepatitis E serology, by contrast with that three-virus panel, is recommended in the workup of suspected drug-induced liver injury.\n\nThe autoimmune limb of the differential is the more treacherous one. Antinuclear antibody and anti-smooth muscle antibody positivity supports a diagnosis of autoimmune hepatitis; such reactivity indicated to a consulting hepatologist that the process was autoimmune, and he attributed the liver injury to underlying autoimmune hepatitis. A normal antinuclear antibody titre excludes autoimmune hepatitis; that exclusionary rule and the diagnostic weight carried by antinuclear and anti-smooth muscle antibody positivity cannot both be true, and the reliance placed on such reactivity rules the former out.\n\nThe treating physician attributed the jaundice to the newly started antibiotic, an attribution that cannot be squared with the hepatologist's assignment of the injury to underlying autoimmune disease, since one agent or the other, but not both accounts, can be the cause. That bedside attribution was recorded first; only afterwards was the RUCAM scale applied, which assigns points for latency, the course of enzyme levels after drug withdrawal, risk factors, and exclusion of competing causes. Only when latency, the response to dechallenge, and the full serological panel are weighed together does any attribution carry conviction.",
